<h2>What the petition sought</h2>
<p>The petitioner invoked Section 11 for the appointment of a sole arbitrator to decide disputes arising from a memorandum of understanding dated 17 April 2018 with a developer company. By the hearing, the battlefield had shrunk to nothing: the respondent&rsquo;s counsel stated there was no objection to an appointment confined to disputes arising from the MoU, and the existence of a valid arbitration clause was not in dispute.</p>

<h2>The court&rsquo;s short path</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Check the clause.</strong> The enquiry, the order records, &ldquo;is confined to examining the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Confirm consensus.</strong> The parties were ad idem on referring the disputes under the MoU to arbitration.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appoint and route.</strong> A member of the Bar was appointed sole arbitrator, to enter reference within three weeks and make Section 12(2) disclosures within three weeks of entering reference, with the arbitration administered under DIAC rules and fees per the DIAC schedule.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Preserve objections.</strong> All pleas, including arbitrability and jurisdiction, were left open before the arbitrator.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why so brief — and why that is the point</h2>
<p>The brevity is doctrinal, not casual. Since the 2015 amendment introduced Section 11(6A) and the Supreme Court&rsquo;s subsequent case law pared back judicial interference at the referral stage, the appointment hearing has become a gateway, not a battleground. Courts look for an arbitration agreement on the face of the record and leave everything else to the tribunal, whose competence to rule on its own jurisdiction is the architecture of the Act. For parties resisting arbitration, the practical consequence is that most defences are not lost but deferred — they must be raised before the arbitrator under Section 16, and a referral order like this one says nothing about their eventual fate.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers for Section 11 petitions in Delhi</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Get the paperwork complete.</strong> The petition should annex the contract containing the clause, the invocation notice under Section 21, and proof of its service — the three things the court actually looks at.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Expect institutional routing.</strong> Delhi High Court appointments commonly direct DIAC administration with its fee schedule, which gives both sides predictability on costs and procedure from day one.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Disclosures are built in.</strong> The appointee&rsquo;s Section 12(2) disclosure requirement is written into the order itself, preserving the parties&rsquo; right to question independence or impartiality at the outset.</div>
</div>
<p>For claimants sitting on stale or disputed MoUs, the decision is an encouragement that the courtroom stage of starting an arbitration is now short. For respondents, it is a reminder that the real contest begins before the tribunal — and that consenting to a confined reference, as happened here, can itself be a sensible way of controlling scope.</p>
